Endpoints & Headers

1) Get JWT (Bank → RBAC via Gateway)

POST /auth/jwt
x-api-key: <BANK_API_KEY>
# mTLS client cert presented at TLS layer
Body: { "scope": "settlement.write" }   # optional

Gateway → RBAC (forwarded):

X-Api-Key-Id: <api_key_id>
X-Client-Cert-Fingerprint: sha256:<...>
X-Request-Id: <uuid>

RBAC → IdP (client credentials):

POST /oauth2/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&
client_id=<oidc_client_id>&
client_secret=<resolved_from_secret_manager>&
scope=settlement.write

RBAC → Bank (via Gateway):

{
  "access_token": "<JWT>",
  "token_type": "Bearer",
  "expires_in": 600
}

2) Protected API call

POST /settlement/v1/submit
Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
x-api-key: <BANK_API_KEY>
# mTLS client cert at TLS

What each component does
	•	API Gateway
	•	Verifies mTLS and API key; resolves/attaches X-Api-Key-Id
	•	Rate limits, quotas, logging; forwards fingerprint header
	•	RBAC Middleware
	•	For /auth/jwt: DB lookup → fetch client_secret from Secret Manager → OIDC token request → return JWT
	•	For protected APIs: verify JWT (JWKS cache), map tenant, enforce RBAC + allowlists (wallets/contracts/methods), audit
	•	Secret Manager
	•	Stores OIDC client_secret; returns on-demand (never logs it)
	•	IdP (OIDC)
	•	Issues short-lived access tokens for client_credentials

Security & Ops tips (for this model)
	•	Do not expose or log client_secret; only RBAC reads from Secret Manager.
	•	Cache the JWT per tenant until expiry; proactively refresh on 80–90% TTL.
	•	Enable CRL/OCSP on Gateway for client-cert revocation.
	•	Use mTLS-bound tokens (RFC 8705) if your IdP supports it (extra strong binding).
	•	Rotate: API keys, client secrets; monitor token issuance rates (abuse detection).
	•	In RBAC, verify JWT claims: iss, aud, exp, nbf, scope/roles, sub.

